<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hortulus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 840</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as De cultura hortorum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hortulus, or On the Cultivation of Gardens, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> poem written around 840 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walafrid-strabo">Walafrid Strabo</a>, abbot of Reichenau on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a>. It describes the labour of the gardener and some two dozen plants of a monastery garden, with their uses in medicine and their symbolic meanings. It is one of the earliest works on gardening from medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p></div>
